+### Control-memory lifetime of host-only command recordings
+
+- **Symptom:** camera-dependent CPU frame times rise while GPU time stays around 12-15 ms, followed by `FATAL NVN command memory: ... host-lent segments reached 1024 MB ...`.
+- **Cause:** `AddControlMemory` was a no-op. An empty command-pool grant has no guest command bytes to anchor borrowed segments, even though its native handles belong to the control-memory range provided by the game. Such segments kept an unknown origin, accumulated permanently, and enlarged the allocator's linear searches. The fatal cap is demonstrated in the private device log; exact on-device CPU attribution remains unmeasured.
+- **Fix:** retain the control-memory grant on the command buffer. Host-only recordings inherit this range, and re-granting an overlapping control range releases its old segments. Ordinary command-memory origins remain authoritative for recording overflows. A new idle grant uses the current control owner instead of the previous host cursor's owner. Finalizing a command-buffer object or elapsed frame count never reclaims cached handles; unknown ownership remains conservative. The 1 GiB cap is unchanged.
+- **Check:** `python3 dm/tools/tests/run_command_memory_native.py --cc clang` compiles the actual production helper bodies and checks 200,000 re-grants bounded to two segments, cached handles with independent lifetimes, overlapping ranges, chained overflow, ordinary command origins, fresh control ownership, and malformed/unknown ranges. This is a single-thread CPU fixture, not gameplay/GPU acceptance. The pre-fix source fails the bounded re-grant assertion.
+- **Found on:** [Super Mario Odyssey](../../dm/games/0100000000010000/README.md), Wooded Kingdom on F6; the user reports F4.1 also performs poorly there. Exact-build local ARM64 inspection shows the game supplies control memory before the empty command-pool grants and keeps native handles after finalizing their command-buffer object. The host correction is general and uses no fixed game addresses.
